WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.080
A seismic wave transfers energy through material.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.900
Individual particles oscillate near their positions;

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.080
they do not travel all the way from the earthquake to

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.520
a recording station. P-waves are compressional body waves.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.620
Particle motion is parallel to wave propagation, and these waves travel

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.980
through solids and liquids. They arrive before S-waves along comparable paths.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.760
S-waves are shear body waves.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.300
Particle motion is perpendicular to propagation.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.180
They travel through solids but cannot propagate as shear waves through

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:28.540
liquid.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.860
Love waves produce horizontal side-to-side motion perpendicular to their travel direction.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:40.300
Rayleigh waves produce rolling, elliptical motion with vertical and horizontal components.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.740
Both are surface-wave types, distinct from P and S body waves.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.420
Surface waves can cause destructive building motion;

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.120
structural response also depends on wave period and building properties.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.660
Amplitude is the size of the displacement.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.060
Period is the time for one full cycle.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.580
Resonance can intensify shaking when the forcing period matches a structure’s

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.460
natural response.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.380
A seismometer senses ground motion.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.960
In the mechanical illustration the frame moves with the ground while

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.340
a suspended mass resists motion through inertia;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.500
their relative movement makes a record.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.300
Three perpendicular components record north–south, east–west, and vertical motion.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.120
A seismogram is the resulting plot of ground motion against time,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.840
not the instrument itself. In the supplied record, background vibrations precede

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.700
P-wave arrival, then S-wave arrival, then larger surface-wave motion.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.180
Increasing source distance generally increases the P–S interval.
